<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Hincks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hincks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Hincks was an Irish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> clergyman, Egyptologist and Assyriologist, known for the decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, he became a Fellow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> in 1813 and from 1826 was rector of Killyleagh, County Down, until his death. Working independently of Henry Creswicke Rawlinson, he deduced the essentially syllabic nature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> cuneiform, and by 1850 he had concluded that Akkadian cuneiform was syllabic and its signs polyphonic, each with several readings. In 1857 he, Rawlinson, Jules Oppert and William Henry Fox Talbot each translated a new inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a>, and the close agreement of their versions settled the decipherment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-hincks.jpg" alt="Edward Hincks" width="330" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Hincks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Edward Hincks</b>, Rawlinson, Jules Oppert and William Henry Fox Talbot worked out its principles in the 1850s, and in 1857 their independent translations of the same inscription confirmed their success.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish scholar <b>Edward Hincks</b> suggested in 1850 that cuneiform had been invented by speakers of a language other than Akkadian, and in 1869 Jules Oppert named it Sumerian.</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li></ul></section>
